==English==
{{was wotd|2016|January|8}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|rāz}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹeɪz/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
*** {{audio|en|en-us-raise.ogg|a=California}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹæɪz/|a=AU,NZ}}
*** {{audio|en|en-au-raze.ogg|a=Queensland}}
* {{homophones|en|raise|rase|rays|rehs|réis|res}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪz|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|rasen}}, {{m|enm|racen}}, {{m|enm|rase|t=to scrape; to shave; to erase; to pull; to strip off; to pluck or tear out; to root out (a tree, etc.); to pull away, snatch; to pull down; to knock down; to rend, tear apart; to pick clean, strip; to cleave, slice; to sever; to lacerate; to pierce; to carve, engrave; to dig; (''figurative'') to expunge, obliterate; to alter}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=rāsen|pos=''v.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED35943}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|raser}}, {{m|xno|rasere}}, {{m|xno|rasser}}, {{der|en|frm|raser}}, and {{der|en|fro|raser|t=to shave; to touch lightly, graze; to level off (grain, etc.) in a measure; to demolish, tear down; to erase; to polish; to wear down}}, from {{der|en|la-vul|*raso|t=to shave; to scrape; to scratch; to touch lightly, graze}}, from {{der|en|la|rāsus|t=scraped; shaved}}, the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|rādō|t=to scrape, scratch; to shave; to rub, smooth; to brush along, graze}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=8012972660|date=March 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> {{doublet|en|rash}} ([[rash#Etymology 2|etymology 2]] and [[rash#Etymology 7|etymology 7]]).

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=† raze|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=7060558275|date=July 2023}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[level#Verb|level]] or [[tear down]] (a [[building#Noun|building]], a [[town]], etc.) to the [[ground#Noun|ground]]; to [[demolish]].
##* {{RQ:Froissart Berners Cronycles|year=1970|volume=1|chapter=Howe the Frẽche Kyng Sent a Great Nauy to the See&#11805; ⁊ howe Duyers Townes were Brent in Englande: ⁊ howe the Duke of Burgoyne Tooke Dyuers Castels about Calys|folio=cxcvii|verso=1|column=1|passage=The fortreſſe was '''raſed''' and beaten downe to the erthe&#11805; whiche had coſt moche the makynge therof: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|act=II|scene=iii|page=103|column=1|passage=Theſe are his ſubſtance, ſinevves, armes, and ſtrength, / VVith vvhich he yoaketh your rebellious Neckes, / '''Razeth''' your Cities, and ſubuerts your Tovvnes, / And in a moment makes them deſolate.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalm|137|7|sig=Hhh2|verso=1|page=717|column=2|passage=Remember, O {{smallcaps|Lord}}, the children of Edom, in the day of Jeruſalem; who ſayd, '''raſe''' it, '''raſe''' it: euen to the foundation thereof.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|book=II|page=22|passage=[N]ovv I find, / Theſe men vvould render thee the ſhame, of all men; nor vvould pay, / Their ovvne vovves to thee, vvhen they tooke, their free and honord vvay, / From Argos hither; that till Troy, vvere by their braue hands '''rac't''', / They vvould not turne home; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Raleigh Mahomet|page=50|passage=For his further ſecuritie he {{quote-gloss|Don Roderigo}} diſarmed his ſubjects; ſuch Caſtles and ſtrengths as hee vvas jealous of vvere '''raced''', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Herbert Temple|chapter=The Sacrifice|page=21|passage=Some ſaid, that I the Temple to the floore / In three dayes '''raz'd''', and raiſed as before.}}
##* {{RQ:Butler Genuine Remains|volume=I|chapter=Rump-Parliament|page=302|passage=[A]fter they had deſtroyed ''Religion'', they pulled dovvn Churches, (as being then of no Uſe) and '''raſed''' the nobleſt Structures in the Land, to ſell the Materials; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1755|author=William Green|chapter=A New Version of the Third Chapter of [[w:Book of Habakkuk|Habakkuk]]|title=A New Translation of the Prayer of Habakkuk, the Prayer of Moses, and the CXXXIX [[w:Psalms|Psalm]];{{nb...|with a Commentary on Each. To which are Added Notes Critical and Explanatory.}}|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher={{...|Printed by}} J[oseph] Bentham, printer to the [[w:University of Cambridge|University]]; [s]old by T. Merrill{{nb...|in Cambridge; J. Whiston and B. White in Fleet-Street; and B. Dod in Ave-Mary-Lane, London; J. Fletcher in Oxford; and J. Hildyard at York.}}|section=verse 18|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5HVQN6PSK6EC&pg=PA10|oclc=1264947221|passage=Thou vvoundeſt the Head of the Houſe of the VVicked; / Thou '''raſedſt''' the Foundation even to the Rock; / Thou piercedſt thro' vvith thy Scepter the Head of the Villages.}}
##* {{RQ:Grose England and Wales|volume=II|chapter=Leeds Castle, Kent|page=114|passage={{quote-gloss|{{w|Edward I of England}}}} cauſed [[w:Henry de Cobham, 1st Baron Cobham|Henry Cobham]], {{...}} to '''race''' the caſtle that Robert de Crevequer had erected, becauſe Crevequer (that vvas the ovvner of it, and heire to Robert) vvas of the number of the nobles that moved and mainteined vvare againſt him; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=II|chapter=Residence of [[w:Julian (emperor)|Julian]] at Antioch—His Successful Expedition against the Persians—{{nb...|Passage of the Tigris—The Retreat and Death of Julian—Election of Jovian—He Saves the Roman Army by a Disgraceful Treaty}}|page=437|passage=The fortifications vvere '''razed''' to the ground; and not a veſtige vvas left, that the city of Maogamalcha had ever existed.}}
##* {{RQ:Prescott Mexico|volume=I|chapter=Decisive Victory—Indian Council—Night Attack—Negotiations with the Enemy—Tlascalan Hero|page=452|passage=He {{quote-gloss|{{w|Hernán Cortés}}}} made the same professions of amity as befo0re, promising oblivion of all past injuries; but, if this proffer was rejected, he would visit their capital as a conqueror, '''raze''' every house in it to the ground, and put every inhabitant to the sword!}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=13 May 2017|author=w:Barney Ronay|title={{w|Antonio Conte}}’s brilliance has turned [[w:Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]’s pop-up team into champions|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427205814/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/may/13/antonio-conte-chelsea-champions-manager|passage=Just as significant in the long term, Chelsea were also granted permission this season for their new on-site mega-stadium, a 60,000-seat upgrade that will mean the current Stamford Bridge is '''razed''' and replaced by something that looks like a vast alien space yurt made of giant Martian redwood stems.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|date=1 December 2018|author=Drachinifel [pseudonym]|title=Anti-Slavery Patrols – The West Africa Squadron|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241129115918/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiSekII0sjw|time=9:45|passage=After his actions were challenged by foreign governments and Parliament initially tried to put a stop to his action, Denman returned home and argued his case with enough force that, by 1848, the Royal Navy was handed active permission and ''encouragement'' to '''raze''' every last slave factory they could find to the ground, and ''full'' authority to stop ''any'' ship, of ''any'' flag, that was thought to be a slaver, with a guarantee with{{sic}} ''no'' censure from the government.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[completely]] [[remove#Verb|remove]] (someone or something), especially from a [[place#Noun|place]], a [[situation#Noun|situation]], etc.; also, to remove from [[existence]]; to [[destroy]], to [[obliterate#Verb|obliterate]].
##: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:destroy}}
##* {{RQ:Lyly Euphues|part=2|chapter=To Philautus|folio=71|passage=Yet at the laſt, caſting with my ſelf, that y&#868; heat of thy loue might cleane be '''razed''' with y&#868; coldnes of my letter, I thought it good to commit an inconuenience, that I might preuent a miſchiefe, chuſing rather to cut thée off ſhort by rigour, then to giue thée any iot of hope by ſilence.}}
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=i|page=57|passage=VVithout reſpect of ſex, degree or age. / He '''raceth''' all his foes vvith fire and ſvvord.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=I|scene=I|sig=C2|page=16|passage=Ile find a day to maſſacre them all, / And '''race''' their faction and their familie, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Defoe Devil|chapter=Of the Devil's Second Kingdom, and How He Got Footing in the Renew'd World by His Victory over {{w|Noah}} and His Race|page=156|passage=It is true, the ''[[w:Devil in Christianity|Devil]]'' did not immediately '''raſe''' out the Notion of Religion and of a {{smallcaps|God}} from the Minds of Men, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:C. Lamb Glenarvon|volume=I|chapter=VIII|page=69|passage=[I]t seemed his desire to '''raze''' every trace of sorrow from the memory of his child; and to conceal the ravages of death under the appearance at least of wild and unceasing gaiety.}}
## {{lb|en|also|figurative}} To [[erase#Verb|erase]] (a [[record#Noun|record]], [[text#Noun|text]], etc.), originally by [[scrape#Verb|scraping]]; to [[rub out]], to [[scratch out]].
##* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Garland|lines=1478–1480|page=420|passage=Suppleyng to Fame, I besought her grace, / And that it wolde please her, full tenderly I prayd, / Owt of her bokis {{w|Apollo}} to '''rase'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Wyatt Poems|poem=Sonnet 31|stanza=1|lines=13–14|page=43|passage=The wound alas happe in some other place, / From whence no toole away the skar can '''race'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Turberville Epitaphes|poem=Take Time|folio=32|verso=1|folioend=33|folioref=33|passage=[D]rowſie drouping Age, / incroaching on apace, / With penſiue Plough will '''raze''' your hue / and Beauties beames deface.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Amoretti|title=GWI|sig=&#91;¶4&#93;|page=8|passage=[N]o malice of ſucceeding daies, / can '''raſe''' thoſe records of thy laſting praiſe.}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=V|canto=IX|stanza=26|page=303|passage=Thus there he ſtood, vvhyleſt high ouer his head, / There vvritten vvas the purport of his ſin, / In cyphers ſtrange, that fevv could rightly read, / BON FONS: but ''bon'' that once had vvritten bin, / VVas '''raced''' out, and ''Mal'' vvas novv put in.}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Fair Maid|page=38|passage=''Goodl''{{quote-gloss|''ack''}}. My name is Captaine ''Thomas Good——'' / ''Beſſ''{{quote-gloss|''e Bridges''}}. I can ſee no good in thee, '''Race''' that ſyllable / Out of thy name.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|sonnet=25|sig=C2|page=43|passage=The painefull vvarrier famoſed for vvorth, / After a thouſand victories once foild, / Is from the booke of honour '''raſed''' quite, / And all the reſt forgot for vvhich he toild: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Of Reformation|year=1916|page=23|passage=[O]f those Books that passe for authentick who knows what hath bin tamper'd withall, what hath bin '''raz'd''' out, what hath bin inserted, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Mixt Contemplations|part=2|chapter=X|page=17|passage=Though we carry a ''ſimple and ſingle remembrance'' of our loſſes unto the grave, it being impoſſible to do other-waies (except we '''raze''' the faculty of memory ''Roote and Branch'' out of our mind) yet let us not keep any record of them with the leaſt ''reflection'' of ''revenge''.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|lines=361–363|sig=B2|verso=1|page=13|passage=Though of their Names in heavenly Records novv / Be no memorial, blotted out and '''ras'd''' / By thir Rebellion, from the Books of Life.}}
##* {{RQ:Wood Life|volume=IV|date=3 March 1693|chapter=Wood's Trial for Libel; and Expulsion from the University. [XXII. Wood's Allegation.]|pages=18–19|pageref=19|passage=That the right honourable [[w:Henry Hyde, 2nd Earl of Clarendon|Henry earl of Clarendon]], the party promovent, had the originall papers, or some of the original papers, {{...}} and altered the aforesaid originall papers, by '''razing''' out many lines, sentences, and words or inserting many lines, sentences and words relating to the character or characters of [[w:Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon|Edward late earl of Clarendon]], without the knowledge or consent of Mr. Wood.}}
##* {{RQ:Ayliffe Juris Canonici|chapter=Of Accusation, and the Course of It|pages=24–25|pageref=25|passage=''Inſcription'' is an Obligation made in VVriting, vvhereby the Accuſer binds himſelf to undergo the ſame Puniſhment, if he ſhall not prove the Crime vvhich he objects to the Party accuſed in his accuſatory Libel, {{...}} And if ſuch Articles are not legally inſcrib'd, as aforeſaid, then the Name of the Defendant ſhall be '''raſed''' out, and the Defendant ſhall be reſtor'd to his former ſtate of Innocence.}}
##* {{RQ:Young Night-Thoughts|night=5|page=33|passage=Our quick-returning ''Folly'' cancels all; / As the Tide ruſhing '''raſes''' vvhat is vvrit / In yielding Sands, and ſmooths the Letter'd Shore.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Marino Faliero|scene=i|page=151|passage=Thy goods are confiscate unto the state, / Thy name is '''razed''' from out her records, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Poetical Works|volume=IV|poem=A Fragment|pages=133|passage=They were two cousins, almost like two twins, / Except that from the catalogue of sins / Nature had '''razed''' their love—which could not be / But by dissevering their nativity.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=[[w:Augustine of Hippo|S[aint] Augustine, Bishop of Hippo]]|chapter=Homily XXIII|translator=[[w:Henry Browne (scholar)|H[enry] Browne]]|title=Homilies on the [[w:Gospel of John|Gospel according to St. John]], and His [[w:First Epistle of John|First Epistle]]{{nb...|by S. Augustine, Bishop of Hippo, Translated, with Notes and Indices. In Two Volumes.}}|series=[[w:Library of the Fathers|A Library of the Fathers of the Holy Catholic Church, anterior to the Division of the East and West]]|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher={{w|John Henry Parker}}; London: [[w:Rivington (publishers)|F[rancis] and J[ohn] Rivington]]|volume=I|page=362|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/homiliesongospel01augu/page/362/mode/1up|oclc=1359918|passage=[W]hy makest thou thine own similitude thy mark, and '''razest''' out the similitude of God within thee?}}
##* {{RQ:Haggard She|page=299|passage=We both loved her now and for all time, she was stamped and carven on our hearts, and no other woman or interest could ever '''raze''' that splendid die.}}
##* {{RQ:Gissing Nether World|volume=II|chapter=A Double Consecration|page=256|passage=Knit your brows against her; shake your head and '''raze''' her name from that catalogue of saints whereon you have inscribed it in anticipation.}}
##* {{RQ:Lawrence New Poems|poem=Suburbs|stanza=1|page=17|passage=O stiffly shapen houses that change not, / What conjuror's cloth was thrown across you, and raised, / To show you thus transfigured, changed, / Your stuff all gone, your menace almost '''rased'''?}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|except|UK|regional}} To [[wound#Verb|wound]] (someone or [[part#Noun|part]] of their [[body#Noun|body]]) [[superficially]]; to [[graze#Verb|graze]].
##* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=24|folio=342|verso=1|passage=[T]he ſvvorde more mercifull then hee to himſelfe, vvith the ſlipping of the pommel, the point ſvvarued, and '''razed''' him but vpon the ſide: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|date=22 February 1685|volume=I|chapter=A Sermon Preached at Westminster-Abbey, February 22. 168{{frac|4|5}}.|page=403|passage=For vvas he not in the neareſt Neighbourhood to Death? And might not the Bullet, that perhaps '''raſed''' his Cheek, have as eaſily gone into his Head?}}
##* {{RQ:Young Busiris|page=68|passage=I cou'd not bear / To '''raze''' thy Skin {{quote-gloss|with a dagger}}, to ſave the VVorld from Ruin.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Marmion|canto=3|stanza=XXVI|page=159|passage=Yet did a splinter of his lance / Through Alexander's visor glance, / And '''razed''' the skin—a puny wound.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To [[alter#Verb|alter]] (a [[document#Noun|document]]) by erasing parts of it.
###* {{RQ:Palsgrave Lesclarcissement|book=3rd|chapter=The Table of Verbes|folio=cccxxxii|column=1|passage=This indenture is '''raced''' all the worlde may ſe it: ''Ceſte indenture eſt faulcée tout le monde le peult veoyr.''}}
###* {{RQ:Marlowe Nashe Dido|sig=A3|page=25|passage=But I vvill take another order novv, / And '''race''' th'eternall Regiſter of time: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Hale Historia Placitorum Coronae|volume=I|chapter=Concerning the New Felonies Enacted in the Times of [[w:Henry IV of England|H{{quote-gloss|enry}} 4.]] [[w:Henry V of England|H{{quote-gloss|enry}} 5.]] [[w:Henry VI of England|H{{quote-gloss|enry}} 6.]] [[w:Edward IV|E{{quote-gloss|dward}} 4.]]|pages=650–651|pageref=650|passage={{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/historiaplacitor01hale/page/650/mode/1up page 650]}} A '''raſing''' or cancelling of a record by the order of that court, in vvhoſe cuſtody the record is, is no felony in him that doth it, nor in the court that commands it, for the court hath a ſuperintendence, as vvell over the record as over the clerks. {{...}} It muſt be ſuch an embezzelling or avoiding of the record, by reaſon vvhereof a judgment is reverſed, {{...}} {{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/historiaplacitor01hale/page/651/mode/1up page 651]}} [I]f ''A. B.'' be ſued by the original to the ''exigent'' and outlavved, and aftervvard the ''exigent'' is made ''C. B.'' and the original is alſo made ''C. B.'' to make all agree, this is felony as vvell in the clerk that '''raſeth''' the original, as him that '''raſeth''' the exigent.}}
### To [[carve#Verb|carve]] (a [[line#Noun|line]], [[mark#Noun|mark]], etc.) into something; to [[incise]], to [[inscribe]]; also, to carve lines, marks, etc., into (something); to [[engrave]].
###* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|chapter=Experiment Solitary, Touching Paintings of the Body|page=191|para=739|passage=Generally, ''Barbarous People'', that goe Naked, doe not onely paint Themſelues, but they povvnce and '''raze''' their Skinne, that the ''Painting'' may not be taken forth; And make it into VVorks.}}
###* {{RQ:Moxon Mechanick Exercises|volume=I|page=17|passage=[Y]ou muſt mark the out-lines of your intended ''Hinge'', {{...}} either vvith Chalk, or elſe '''raſe''' upon the Plate vvith the corner of the ''Cold-Chiſſel'', or any other hardned Steel that vvill ſcratch a bright ſtroke upon the Plate: {{...}}}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1759|author=w:Isaac Ambrose|chapter=Dooms-day|title=The Compleat Works of that Eminent Minister of God’s Word Mr. Isaac Ambrose,{{nb...|Consisting of These Following Treatises, viz. Prima, Media, et Ultima, or, The First, Middle, and Last Things. Wherein is Set Forth: I. The Doctrine of Regeneration, or the New Birth. II. Of Sanctification, in the Means, Duties, and Ordinances, both Private and Publick, for Continuance and Increase of a Godly Life. III. Certain Meditations on Man’s Misery, in His Life, Death, Judgment; and on Heaven and Hell; as also, on God’s Mercy, in His Redemption, and Salvation. With a Sermon Added, Concerning Redeeming the Time, Looking unto Jesus, as Carrying on the Great Work of Man’s Salvation. War with Devils; Ministration of, and Communion with Angels.}}|location=Dundee, Scotland|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Henry Galbraith and Company|page=304|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zFFgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA304|column=1|oclc=976885840|passage=This vvas the heart thou piercedſt, theſe are the vvounds thou '''razedſt''', and this is the blood thou ſpilledſt: {{...}}|footer={{small|Applied to the causing of wounds on {{w|Jesus}}'s body. The word was spelled ''raced'' in ''Prima & Ultima: The First & Last Thinges or Regeneration and Medita''[''t'']''ion Sermõs in Two Treatises'' (1st edition, 1640), [https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_prima-ultima-the-firs_ambrose-isaac_1640/page/n238/mode/1up page 232].}}}}
### To remove (something) by scraping; also, to [[cut#Verb|cut]] or [[shave#Verb|shave]] (something) off.
### To [[rub#Verb|rub]] [[lightly]] along the [[surface#Noun|surface]] of (something); [[brush#Verb|brush]] against, to graze.
###* {{RQ:Marcellinus Holland Roman Historie|chapter=III|chaptername=Warre against the Lentienses, a People of Alemaine. The Description of the Lake Brigantia. The Romane Armie Discomfited and Put to Flight, having within a while after Vanquished the Alemans, Returned to Millaine, there to Winter|page=33|passage=And novv {{quote-gloss|the Rhine}} by this time augmented vvith ſnovv, melted and reſolved into vvater, and '''raſing''' as it goes the high bankes vvith their curving reaches, entreth into a round and vaſt lake (vvhich the Rhætians dvvelling thereby, call Brigantia) {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Beckford Vathek|page=103|passage=Sometimes, his feet '''raſed''' the ſurface of the water; and, at others, the ſkylight almoſt flattened his noſe.}}
### To scrape (something), with or as if with a [[razor#Noun|razor]], to remove [[thing]]s from its [[surface#Noun|surface]]; also, to [[reduce]] (something) to [[small#Adjective|small]] [[piece#Noun|pieces]] by scraping; to [[grate#Verb|grate]].
###* {{RQ:Jonson Gypsies|page=65|passage=And you are a ſoule, ſo vvhite, and ſo chaſte, / A table ſo ſmooth, and ſo nevvly '''ra'ſte''', / As nothing cald foule, / Dare approach vvith a blot, / Or any leaſt ſpot; {{...}}}}
### To shave (someone or part of their body) with a [[razor#Noun|razor]], etc.
###* {{RQ:Lyly Euphues|part=2|chapter=Euphues to Him, that was His Philautus|folio=82|verso=1|passage=[A] ſharpe worde moued thée, when other whiles a ſworde will not, then a friendly checke killeth thée, when a raſor cannot '''raſe''' thée.}}
###* {{RQ:Evelyn Publick Employment|pages=95–96|pageref=95|passage=Do you fancy him retir'd that {{...}} conſumes his time trifling amongſt ''Barbers'', '''razing''' and ſprucing himſelf, Povvdering, Combing, and ſummoning a Council upon every ''Hair''?}}
### {{lb|en|also|figurative}} To [[cut#Verb|cut]], [[scratch#Verb|scratch]], or [[tear#Verb|tear]] (someone or something) with a [[sharp#Adjective|sharp]] [[object#Noun|object]]; to [[lacerate#Verb|lacerate]], to [[slash#Verb|slash]].
###* {{RQ:Ascham Toxophilus|edition=new|folio=38|passage=Buckles and agglettes at vnwares, ſhall '''race''' his bowe, a thinge both euill for the fight, ⁊ perillous for freatinge.}}
